::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display .cell-output-markdown}
Local Moran statistics (LISA) of **Sustainable Development Index, IDS (0-100)** were computed in 2012 and in 2024 for the same 343 units, under queen contiguity (1 island(s) attached to nearest neighbor), row-standardized, n=343, and each unit was assigned to a type at the 0.05 level. Global Moran's I is 0.432 in 2012 (pseudo p = 0.001) and 0.468 in 2024 (pseudo p = 0.001): the overall clustering of similar values changed little.

**275 of 343 units (80%) are of the same type in both years** and 68 changed type. Of the 104 units in a significant cluster or outlier in 2012, 71 kept their type, 23 are no longer significant in 2024 and 10 switched to another type. 35 units that were not significant in 2012 belong to a cluster or are an outlier in 2024.

Of the 46 **High-High** hot spots (high values surrounded by high values) of 2012, 39 are still High-High in 2024, a year with 60 of them. Of the 44 **Low-Low** cold spots (low values surrounded by low values) of 2012, 30 are still Low-Low in 2024, a year with 45 of them. The most common change is from Not significant to High-High (17 units).

Read the changes with care. A unit's type depends on whether its pseudo p-value is below the threshold, computed unit by unit without a multiple-testing adjustment, so moves to and from Not significant can come from borderline p-values. High and low are relative to the mean of each year, and a unit can change type because its neighbours' values changed while its own did not.

_These are associations, not causal effects. A causal reading needs a research design._
:::

